    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Four circles of radius 1 are touching each other and a smaller circle as seen in the picture. What is the radius of the smaller circle?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2013-g1112/q02.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\sqrt2 - 1$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac12$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\frac34$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac{7}{16}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Diagonal of the square of centres",
        "body": "The centres of the four big circles form a square of side 2 (neighbouring circles touch). Its diagonal is $2\\sqrt2$. Along the diagonal we pass radius 1, the small circle's diameter $2r$, and radius 1: $1 + 2r + 1 = 2\\sqrt2$, so $r = \\sqrt2 - 1$.\n\n**Answer: (A)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Distance to the centre",
        "body": "The small circle is centred at the centre of the square of centres. The distance from there to a big circle's centre is half the diagonal, $\\sqrt2$, and must equal $1 + r$. So $r = \\sqrt2 - 1 \\approx 0.41$.\n\n**Answer: (A)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 1,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
