    "points": 4,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The diagram shows two squares of equal side length placed so that they overlap. The squares have a common vertex and the sides make an angle of 45 degrees with each other, as shown. What is the area of the overlap as a fraction of the area of one square?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2013-g1112/q11.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\frac12$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\sqrt2 - 1$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac{\\sqrt2 - 1}{2}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "A kite of two right triangles",
        "body": "Take side length 1 and let $O$ be the common vertex. The overlap is a kite $OPQR$: $OR$ is a side of the upright square, $OP$ a side of the tilted square (both of length 1), and $Q$ is where the two other sides cross. The angles at $P$ and $R$ are right angles (corners of the squares), so the kite consists of two congruent right triangles $OPQ$ and $ORQ$ with hypotenuse $OQ$.\n\nThe angle $\\angle ROP = 45^\\circ$ is bisected by $OQ$, so $RQ = \\tan 22.5^\\circ = \\sqrt2 - 1$. Area of the kite $= 2\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot 1\\cdot(\\sqrt2 - 1) = \\sqrt2 - 1$, and this is also the fraction of the square's area.\n\n**Answer: (D)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Coordinates",
        "body": "Put $O = (0,0)$, the upright square $[0,1]\\times[0,1]$, and the tilted square with sides along $y = x$ and $y = -x$ from $O$ (side 1, so its right corner is $P = (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2})$). Its far side $y = -x + \\sqrt2$ meets $y = 1$ at $Q = (\\sqrt2 - 1, 1)$. The overlap is the quadrilateral $O, P, Q, R = (0,1)$. With $a = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}$ and $b = \\sqrt2 - 1$ the shoelace formula gives\n\n$$\\text{Area} = \\tfrac12\\left(a\\cdot 1 - b\\cdot a + b\\cdot 1\\right) = \\tfrac12\\left(a(1 - b) + b\\right) = \\tfrac12\\left((\\sqrt2 - 1) + (\\sqrt2 - 1)\\right) = \\sqrt2 - 1,$$\n\nusing $a(1-b) = \\frac{2 - \\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2} = \\sqrt2 - 1$.\n\n**Answer: (D)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry",
      "trigonometry"
    ],
